\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Hey, it\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s your #1 fan. If you\u8217\'92re ever wondering if your writing will reach its audience\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 yes. It\u8217\'92s me.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Anyways. Here\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s why this was horrible (I kid.)}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 I did read ch.1 prior to this critique.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 First, I want to mention the Vitae Patrum, which I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92m betting you\u8217\'92ve read. If not, there\u8217\'92s a whole lot of juicy language for inspiration. e.g.: }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 >}{\f0\fs24\b0 After they had all gone a beautiful prostitute came in, and began stroking his body in the most delicate of embraces, and, shameful to relate, fondling his manhood in her hands, in the hope of so inciting him to lust that she might win a shameless victory over him.}{ }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Next, a quick note I have on the style. This thought happened in chapter 1, but did not continue all the way through chapter 2:}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 I think the contrast between the Augustan narrative style and the modern setting could be pushed, maybe even into hysterics. The character\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s speech, potentially, is a place for it. Like, instead of the crazy woman \u8216\'91sirring\u8217\'92 him, use \u8216\'91dude\u8217\'92 and \u8216\'91bro\u8217\'92. It\u8217\'92s a lot of fun (for me) to hear the elevated narration right next to the most mundane shit. An extreme example:}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 > \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93And what is this curse?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 > \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Being horny.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 >Shame stung me stiff. I thought for a moment that she was a holy fool, a mind-reading saint posing as a homeless woman, and that she had come to reproach me for my ill-regulated desires, and for uncharity toward the poor. I told myself I wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t important enough to merit such a visitation and did my best to laugh inwardly.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 >\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Bitch, I don\u8217\'92t know you.\u8221\'94 I had failed already\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 I hadn\u8217\'92t mentioned the Church.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Obviously, this is a different vibe than what you\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92ve got going on, especially in chapter 2, but I just wanted to mention it because when I think about how far this could be pushed, I immediately start getting ideas and want to write it myself and so I thought, maybe, it might kickstart some things for you?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Please ignore all that if not; I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92m supposed to be engaging with what you\u8217\'92re wanting to do instead of what I, subjectively, want you to do.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Anyways, Ch 2:}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 I am in love with the juxtaposition of the character\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s thoughts and the active conversation. It really captured how down bad a man (or woman) can be. So, so, so good. It\u8217\'92s weird because, it feels like such an obvious device, that direct, consistent juxtaposition, and I feel like I *must* have seen it before, but I can\u8217\'92t recall anything specific. Are you pulling from something here? Regardless, kudos.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 >\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93So,\u8221\'94 I said, staring into Frank\u8217\'92s eyes, \u8220\'93how did you two meet?\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Okay, here\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s a question. I would be interested to hear your thoughts. The subtext behind \u8216\'91staring into Frank\u8217\'92s eyes\u8217\'92, is \u8216\'91purposefully avoiding her eyes\u8217\'92. E.g. this *could* be written as  }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 >\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93So,\u8221\'94 I said, staring into Frank\u8217\'92s eyes, avoiding hers, \u8220\'93how did you two meet?\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 This latter option is clearer\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 even the most inattentive reader will get it. But it is also blunt, and maybe we can even say that it is redundant and stilted, because there is enough there to suggest this meaning to the active reader. On the other hand, clarity is king. I find myself in this situation often and don\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t have a good rule of thumb to help guide the decision.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 >}{\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93}{\f0\fs24\b0 Ioane and Julia,\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94 I said, priestly, my head stuffed with wool, \u8220\'93your imaginations are sadly impoverished. At the Last Judgment, we\u8217\'92ll all be restored to communion with God, and His love will overcome us. What that\u8217\'92ll be like, who can say? It\u8217\'92s ineffable, transcendent. Those who\u8217\'92ve prepared themselves for it will love it, and those who haven\u8217\'92t will hate it.\u8221\'94 I grinned. \u8220\'93But not one of them will escape. His love will pursue them and engulf them like fire, and they\u8217\'92ll be compelled to accept it in spite of their protestation. And they\u8217\'92ll cry out in impotent rage forever and ever.\u8221\'94}
\par\pard\plain \ltrch\loch {\line Isaac the Syrian :). Or maybe, Gregory of Nyssa. Love your expression of it. Looking forward to where it goes. I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92m assuming that, the line \u8220\'93}{\f0\fs24\b0 T}{\f0\fs24\b0 o Death, the mercy of God!\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94 is setting up his route out of the LUST problem, or at least, he flirts with the idea.}
\par\plain 
\par\plain 
\par\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 My only real crit, and this is a fucking HARD one to give because it\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s one of those notes which, for me, always feels like torture to fix (if indeed it needs fixing):}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  The philosophy convo does not feel well motivated.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Narratively, we\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92re deep in the lust, it\u8217\'92s HIGH heat, and then all of a sudden I\u8217\'92m hit with, \u8220\'93Was it not the theories of the renowned philosopher Julius Evola?\u8221\'94 If you can figure out a way to smooth that transition out, where the lust suddenly disappears and is replaced by the philosophy, the chapter is choice. When these things happen in my own writing, its a symptom of the fact that I\u8217\'92ve stitched two separate bits of writing together and neglected the seam. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
